<PageLayout title="Editorial Policy" description="How TechOrbit researches, writes, checks and corrects its articles.">
  <p>Last updated: 17 November 2026</p>
  <h2>Accuracy and sources</h2>
  <p>We check important facts against more than one independent source where possible, and we list our sources at the end of each article. We prefer official announcements, documentation and primary sources over rumors.</p>
  <h2>Originality</h2>
  <p>We write our own explanations and analysis. We do not copy other publishers' articles or rewrite them sentence by sentence. Direct quotes are short, attributed and linked.</p>
  <h2>Testing claims</h2>
  <p>We say we tested a product only when we really did. When an article is based on research and published specifications, we say so clearly.</p>
  <h2>Use of AI</h2>
  <p>We may use AI tools to help with research, outlines and first drafts. AI output is never published automatically. A person checks facts, edits the text and approves each article before publication.</p>
  <h2>Breaking news</h2>
  <p>We do not publish unconfirmed breaking news. If a story is still developing, we say what is confirmed and what is not.</p>
  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>When we find or are told about a mistake, we fix it and note the update on the article. To report an error, see our <a href="/contact/">Contact page</a>.</p>
  <h2>Independence</h2>
  <p>Advertising, affiliate links and sponsorships do not decide what we write or what we recommend. Sponsored content is clearly labeled. See our <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">Affiliate Disclosure</a>.</p>
</PageLayout>
